    <statement><p><p>For each table, say whether <m>y</m> is directly proportional to <m>x</m>, inversely proportional to <m>x</m>, or neither.</p><p>Table 1</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>x</m>: Iron sheets bought</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{2}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{3}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{5}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{8}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>y</m>: Cost (Ksh)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{1840}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{2760}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{4600}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{7360}</m></cell></row></tabular><p><m>y</m> is [ directly proportional | inversely proportional | neither ]  </p><p>Table 2</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>x</m>: Number of workers</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{2}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{4}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{6}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{8}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>y</m>: Days to weed the shamba</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{60}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{30}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{15}</m></cell></row></tabular><p><m>y</m> is [ directly proportional | inversely proportional | neither ]  </p><p>Table 3</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>x</m>: Days the tent is hired</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{1}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{2}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{3}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{5}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>y</m>: Charge (Ksh)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{3500}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{5000}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{6500}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{9500}</m></cell></row></tabular><p><m>y</m> is [ directly proportional | inversely proportional | neither ]  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>For each pair work out <m>y \div x</m> and <m>x \times y</m>. If <m>y \div x</m> is the same every time, it is direct proportion; if <m>x \times y</m> is the same every time, it is inverse proportion; if neither stays the same, it is neither.</p><p>Table 1: <m>y \div x</m>: <m>{920,\; 920,\; 920,\; 920}</m>; <m>x \times y</m>: <m>{3680,\; 8280,\; 23\,000,\; 58\,880}</m>. The ratio stays the same, so <m>y = {920}x</m>: directly proportional.</p><p>Table 2: <m>y \div x</m>: <m>{30,\; 7.5,\; \approx 3.33,\; 1.875}</m>; <m>x \times y</m>: <m>{120,\; 120,\; 120,\; 120}</m>. The product stays the same, so <m>xy = {120}</m>: inversely proportional.</p><p>Table 3: <m>y \div x</m>: <m>{3500,\; 2500,\; \approx 2166.67,\; 1900}</m>; <m>x \times y</m>: <m>{3500,\; 10\,000,\; 19\,500,\; 47\,500}</m>. Neither stays the same: <m>y</m> rises with <m>x</m>, but there is a starting amount, so <m>y</m> is not <m>0</m> when <m>x</m> is <m>0</m>. Neither.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p>directly proportional</p></answer>
    <answer><p>inversely proportional</p></answer>
    <answer><p>neither</p></answer>
</stack-static>